
02/15/2026
for accessing aws from command line

1. Create AWS IAM access  keys for CLI use
   a. Login to console.aws.amazon.com/iam/.
   b. Select Users in the left navigation, then your target users
   c. Go to the Security credentials tab.
   d. Access keys, choose Create access key > Command Line Interface (CLI) > Next
   e. Add optional tags, then Create access key
   f. Show or Download .csv

2. cmd> aws configure
   Fill "AWS Access Key ID"
   Fill "AWS Secret Access Key"

What is running?
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1. See ec2 instance
   cmd> aws ec2 describe-instances --filters "Name=instance-state-name,Values=running" --query "Reservations[*].Instances[*].InstanceId" --output text

2. See ECS services:
   cmd> aws ecs list-clusters
   cmd> aws ecs list-services --cluster <your-cluster>

3. Lambda functions:
   cmd> aws lambda list-functions --query "Functions[?State=='Active'].FunctionName"

4. EBS volumes attached
   cmd> aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters "Name=status,Values=available,in-use"

5. All Service Quotas services
   cmd> aws service-quotas list-services --query "Services[].ServiceName" --output text

6. Account Wide Usage Check
   cmd> aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-02-01,End=2026-02-15 --granularity MONTHLY --metrics "UnblendedCost"
  

Disabling Lambda
=================
Lambda > Your function > Configuration > Concurrency > Edit > Reserve concurrency: 0 > Save. No one can call it now (even manual Test/CLI invokes fail). To re-enable: set to 1+ or delete: aws lambda delete-function --function-name hello-world-test.

